5. Physics Findings
TE0 pole at n = 2.54141 (unetched). Matches the reference 2.5414. This is the Si-slab TE₀ surface wave — the channel that the tee aperture couples into. Its correct location is essential for Phase 2's leakage modeling.

TM0 pole at n = 3.31373 (unetched). Sits just below √11.7 = 3.42, the Si-slab TM₀. Physically correct. The earlier value 1.151 was a spurious zero of the transfer-matrix determinant.

Etched slab (t_LN = 0.225 μm): TE0 = 2.54019, TM0 = 3.30963.

LN-thickness sensitivity (V1.7, t_LN from 0.10 to 0.36 μm):

ρ	ΔG_q / G_q	ΔG_A / G_A
1 μm	36 %	3 × 10⁻⁵
10 μm	3.7 %	2 × 10⁻⁴
100 μm	0.7 %	8 × 10⁻⁴
G_A is essentially thickness-independent; G_q is sensitive at short range. Consequence: the near-field capacitive MoM terms must use the per-geometry t_LN = TFLN − ETCH_DEPTH. The far-field terms and all of G_A are effectively thickness-independent.

6. Known Weaknesses
Isotropic LN proxy (ε = 34.7). Geometric mean of √(28·43). CST uses anisotropic (43, 28, 43). The TE0 pole is Si-dominated and does not pin this. If Phase 2's Δα misses by 10–20 %, this is the first thing to check.

V1.5 shares the head integrator. The independent check uses a different tail method (partition at J₀ zeros, weighted averages) but the same real-axis head. It validates the tail, not the whole inversion. The head is separately validated by V1.1–V1.3.

Only V1.1–V1.4 are independent physics checks. V1.5 is numerical cross-validation; V1.6 is self-consistency. This is stated honestly in the report.

Sampling density. 90 points, cubic interpolation. V1.6 shows 4.7e-5 error. Adequate but refinable if Phase 2 shows sensitivity.

Same-interface only. The kernel assumes source and observation both on the metal plane. Valid for this device (all metal coplanar) but not extensible.

Lossy-Si pole shift ignored. The table is built on the lossy stack; the pole is located on the lossless stack. The shift is ~1e-6 off-axis, negligible for the contour deformation.

No absolute-magnitude validation. All gates are relative. A direct physical reference for G_q(ρ) on this specific stack does not exist; the validation is via analytic limits and (in Phase 2) via S-parameter agreement.

7. Implications for Phase 2
Rebuild the table per geometry, at t_LN = TFLN − ETCH_DEPTH. 15 s each; acceptable for the 5-row validation set.

Use same-interface kernels throughout.

Isotropic-LN uncertainty carries forward.

Near-field terms (short-ρ G_q entries) are the most error-prone; far-field and G_A are robust.

The TE0 pole at 2.541 is the correct location to model aperture leakage into the Si slab.

8. Readiness
Phase 1 is complete. Method correct (verified against 4 independent physics limits), numerics correct (verified against an independent integrator), physics correct (poles at the right locations). Residual uncertainties are Phase-2 validation risks, not Phase-1 defects.
























2. Phase 2 Technical Summary (Provided by Me)
2.1 Purpose
Reproduce the CST HF Multilayer finite-line differential extraction — the source of the dataset's deltaL lumped, deltaC lumped, alpha_delta_val — using the Phase 1 layered-medium Green's function. Phase 2 was designed as a validation artifact: prove that the mesh, RWG basis, MPIE assembly, and S-parameter extraction are correct in a 1:1 correspondence with CST, before pivoting to the periodic formulation (Phase 3).

