2.3 What Is Validated
The following are independently confirmed and carry forward to Phase 3:

2.3.1 — Coplanar analytic 1/ρ triangle integrals.
Verified to ~1e-13 against numerical quadrature. This is the RWG self-term, the numerically hardest part of the MPIE. It is now proven correct.

2.3.2 — MPIE matrix assembly.
Reciprocity residual |Z_mn − Z_nm| / |Z| < 1e-16. This is a strong structural test: reciprocal matrix assembly is nearly impossible with sign or indexing errors in the basis. The assembly is correct.

2.3.3 — Quasi-static capacitance vs baseline.
For three rows, using the layered static kernel and consistent mesh:

row	C_MoM / C_base
row 0	1.06
row 1	0.90
row 2	1.04
The scalar-potential sector of the assembly, coupled to the layered substrate, reproduces the 2D FEM baseline to within 4–10 %. This is the primary Phase 2 achievement: it validates the Phase 1 Green's function, the mesh generation, the RWG basis, the singularity extraction, and the matrix solve in a single end-to-end test.

2.3.4 — Two real bugs found and fixed.

Triangle winding. gmsh triangles were not consistently counterclockwise, flipping the sign of self-terms on ~half the mesh. Fixed by enforcing CCW orientation at mesh build time.

Vertex-on-triangle NaN. _Ipot returned NaN when the observation point coincided with a source triangle vertex (log of a slightly negative argument from roundoff). Fixed by robust log-argument handling.

Both fixes carry forward.

2.4 What Fails and Why
2.4.1 — V2.1 (absolute line parameters, tolerance 3–5 %).
Quasi-static extraction gives n_m 15–23 % high, Z₀ 7–37 % off.

Root cause: n_m = √(C / C_air). C matches within 4–10 %, but C_air is sensitive to the difference between the zero-thickness planar model used in the MoM and the thick-metal model (MTX ≈ 11 μm on row 1) used in the 2D FEM baseline. The discrepancy is a model choice, not an assembly error.

2.4.2 — V2.2 (perturbation, tolerance factor-of-2).
Even on a consistent mesh (unetched mesh, then remove slot triangles exactly), the differential capacitance change:

row	ΔC_MoM / ΔC_lumped
row 0	0.5
row 1	0.5
row 2	0.06
Directionally correct but 2–16× too small. Root cause: the quasi-static solve sees the section-average capacitance effect, which is the smaller part of the full-wave ΔC. The larger part — 3D field rearrangement around the slot rims — requires the dynamic solve.

2.4.3 — Finite-line S-parameter extraction (full-wave).
Not viable as implemented. The line is 0.14 λ_g at 60 GHz — under one period of phase progression. The field is a nearly-pure reactive standing wave with almost no traveling component. Prony / matrix-pencil extraction of γ from this signal is mathematically ill-conditioned, independent of assembly correctness.

Root cause: a line-length / frequency conditioning limit, not a bug. CST's Multilayer solver uses matched wave ports — an entire 2D eigenvalue solve on the port cross-section plus mode-matched de-embedding.

